# Week 5 Report: Analysis of Economic Indicators and Market Trends

**Indicators studied:** economic growth (GDP), employment, inflation and interest rates

**Prepared by:** Nayana Sheelavant        **Date:** 19th October, 2026

## 1. Executive Summary

This report looks at four economic indicators that matter most to stock markets, what each is saying right now, and how they have related to stock returns in the past. I then use them to build a view of the next six to twelve months.

The picture today is unusual. Stocks are at record highs: the S&P 500 closed at 7,811.54 on 9 October 2026, up 14.1% for the year. Yet the economic data underneath is mixed and, in places, uncomfortable. Growth is steady (US GDP rose 2.2% annualised in the second quarter). Hiring has nearly stalled (only 29,000 jobs were added in September). Inflation is re-accelerating (3.7% in September, driven by a 34% annual jump in gasoline). And the Federal Reserve has started raising interest rates again, which has pushed the 10-year Treasury yield to about 5.2%, a level last seen more than two decades ago.

When I tested how these indicators have lined up with stock returns over 2018 to 2024, the clearest link was with interest rates (a correlation of -0.80 between yearly changes in the Fed funds rate and S&P 500 returns). Growth, inflation and unemployment showed almost no link in annual data, which is itself a lesson: markets look ahead, so they often move before the data does. That result rests on only seven years and is driven largely by 2022, so I treat it as an illustration and not as proof.

My base case is for choppy, range-bound markets with wide gaps between winners and losers, because strong earnings are fighting against the highest yields in years. The main risks to that view are an energy price spike and an inflation surprise on one side, and a sudden labour market crack on the other. Section 8 sets out the scenarios and what would tell me which one is unfolding.

