## 3. Economic Growth

### 3.1 What the data says

US real GDP grew at an annualised 2.2% in the second quarter of 2026, according to the third estimate published on 30 September. That was revised up sharply from 1.5% in the earlier estimates, mostly because investment, consumer spending and government spending came in stronger than first thought. The first quarter was revised to 2.5%. Gross domestic income, an alternative measure built from incomes, grew 2.6%.

The longer run is bumpy. Growth was negative in the first quarter of 2025 (-0.5%), jumped to 3.8% and 4.4% in the middle of 2025, then dropped to 0.5% at the end of the year before settling near 2.2% to 2.5% in 2026. Quarterly figures swing a lot and get revised, so the trend over several quarters is more useful than any single number.

