### 3.2 The global picture

The IMF's October 2026 World Economic Outlook expects world growth to slow from 3.5% in 2025 to 3.1% in 2026, then recover to 3.4% in 2027. For the United States it projects 2.3% growth in both 2026 and 2027, supported by consumer spending, fiscal policy and heavy investment in artificial intelligence. The IMF also flags two things I found important. Growth has become very concentrated, with 32 countries producing about 95% of it, and energy-importing and lower-income economies are struggling with higher fuel and food costs after the Middle East conflict.

### 3.3 How growth affects markets

Growth drives company revenue and profit, so faster growth normally supports share prices. The relationship is not simple, though. Markets price the future, so what matters is growth compared with expectations, and growth that is too strong can hurt if it makes the central bank raise rates. In 2026 this tension is visible: solid growth is part of the reason the Fed was willing to hike.

## 4. Employment

### 4.1 What the data says

The September jobs report, released on 2 October, was weak. Nonfarm payrolls rose by only 29,000 against forecasts of around 85,000 to 90,000, and July and August were revised down by a combined 60,000. The three-month average is now only about 51,000 jobs a month, and for all of 2025 payrolls averaged just 15,000 a month. The unemployment rate rose to 4.2% from 4.1%, though the labour force also grew, with participation at 61.8%. Wage growth was about 3% over the year, now below inflation.

Not everything was soft. The broader unemployment measure, which includes discouraged and part-time workers, fell to 7.6%, its lowest since early 2025. So the labour market is cooling and not breaking. Hiring is slow, but layoffs are not surging.

### 4.2 How employment affects markets

Jobs matter in two ways. Strong employment supports consumer spending, which is most of the US economy, so a weak labour market is a warning for profits. But because the Fed watches jobs closely, weak data can also lower expectations of rate rises and lift stocks in the short term. The market reaction to the September report showed this. The weak number cut the market-implied odds of an October Fed hike from roughly 70% to about 14% within the week. Bad news was briefly treated as good news.

## 5. Inflation

### 5.1 What the data says

The BLS reported on 14 October that consumer prices rose 0.6% in September and 3.7% over the year, up from 3.4% in August and 3.0% a year earlier, and above the 3.6% economists expected. Core inflation, which strips out food and energy, was 2.6% over the year and 0.3% for the month. The headline jump came mostly from gasoline, which rose 9.1% in a single month and 34.2% over the year, with fuel oil up 74% and airline fares up 22%.

One economist pointed out that core inflation ran at a 3.7% annualised pace in September, so the underlying trend is not as calm as the 2.6% annual figure implies. Shelter rose 0.4% in the month. The IMF expects US inflation of 3.6% for 2026, easing to 2.8% in 2027, and global inflation of 4.7% in 2026.

### 5.2 How inflation affects markets

Inflation hurts stocks mainly through the central bank. Higher inflation leads to higher interest rates, which reduce the present value of future profits. It also squeezes company margins and household budgets. A moderate level of inflation can be absorbed, since companies with pricing power pass costs on. The danger is a surprise, and the September number was a surprise on the upside, with markets now pricing a rate rise before the end of the year at better than 96% odds.

## 6. Interest Rates

### 6.1 What the data says

The Federal Reserve raised its target range for the federal funds rate by 0.25 percentage points to 3.75% to 4.00% on 16 September 2026. It was the first increase since July 2023 and the vote was unanimous. The median projection has the rate at 4.1% by the end of 2026, which implies one more hike, with no change in 2027 and cuts only in 2028 and 2029. After the September CPI report, markets moved to price a further increase before year-end with high probability. The next meeting is on 27 and 28 October.

Long-term yields have risen even more than the policy rate. The 10-year Treasury yield was around 4.05% in October 2025 and had reached about 5.24% by 9 October 2026, after touching roughly 5.27% earlier that week, which is about the highest level in more than two decades. With inflation at 3.7%, the 10-year yield sits only about 1.5 percentage points above current inflation, so the so-called real yield is positive and meaningful again.

### 6.2 How interest rates affect markets

Rates are the most direct link between the economy and stock prices. A higher rate lowers the value of all future profits, and growth companies, whose profits lie further in the future, are hit hardest. Higher yields also give investors a competing safe return: a risk-free 5% looks attractive next to an S&P 500 earnings yield that is much lower. For banks, higher rates can help, since they earn more on loans and deposits, but they also raise the risk of borrowers failing to repay.

## 7. Relating the Indicators to Stock Returns

### 7.1 Historical episodes

Before looking at statistics, it helps to see the pattern in a few well-known episodes. The figures below are rounded from standard sources.

| Episode | What the indicators did | What stocks did | Lesson |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 2000 to 2002 | The Fed raised rates to 6.5% in 2000. Growth slowed and a recession followed in 2001. | The Nasdaq fell about 78% from its March 2000 peak. The S&P 500 lost about 38% over 2000 to 2002. | High valuations plus rising rates are a dangerous mix. |
| 2008 | GDP contracted, unemployment rose from about 5% to 10% by late 2009. | The S&P 500 fell 37% in 2008 and about 57% from peak to trough. | A collapse in growth and credit hits all stocks. |
| 2020 | GDP fell sharply and unemployment spiked to 14.7% in April. The Fed cut rates to near zero. | The S&P 500 fell about 34% in five weeks, then recovered to a new high by August and returned 18% for the year. | Markets look past bad data when policy support arrives. |
| 2022 | Inflation peaked at 9.1%. The Fed raised rates from near zero to 4.25% to 4.50% in a year. | The S&P 500 fell about 18% in 2022. Bonds fell about 13%. | Rapid rate rises hurt stocks and bonds together. |

*Table 3. Four episodes. Figures rounded.*

Two things stand out. In 2020 the data looked terrible and stocks still rose, because investors expected the recovery and a flood of policy support. In 2022 the economy was not collapsing, yet stocks fell sharply, because rates were rising fast. In both cases the direction of policy mattered more than the level of growth.

### 7.2 A simple statistical check

For a more systematic look, I lined up seven calendar years, 2018 to 2024, and measured how each indicator moved against the S&P 500's total return that year. I stopped at 2024 because it is the last full year for which I could confirm every series. Inputs are rounded from the BEA, BLS, Federal Reserve and S&P Dow Jones Indices.

| Year | S&P 500 total return | Real GDP growth | CPI (Dec to Dec) | Change in unemployment (pts) | Change in Fed funds (pts) | Change in 10-year yield (pts) |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2018 | -4.4% | 2.9% | 1.9% | -0.2 | +1.00 | +0.29 |
| 2019 | 31.5% | 2.3% | 2.3% | -0.3 | -0.75 | -0.77 |
| 2020 | 18.4% | -2.2% | 1.4% | +3.1 | -1.50 | -0.99 |
| 2021 | 28.7% | 6.1% | 7.0% | -2.8 | 0.00 | +0.59 |
| 2022 | -18.1% | 2.5% | 6.5% | -0.4 | +4.25 | +2.36 |
| 2023 | 26.3% | 2.9% | 3.4% | +0.2 | +1.00 | 0.00 |
| 2024 | 25.0% | 2.8% | 2.9% | +0.4 | -1.00 | +0.69 |

