**Figure 2** (chart in the Word version). Annual S&P 500 total return against the change in the Fed funds rate and against real GDP growth, 2018 to 2024. Dashed lines are straight-line fits.

| Year | S&P 500 return (%) | Change in Fed funds (pts) | GDP growth (%) |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2018 | -4.4 | 1.00 | 2.9 |
| 2019 | 31.5 | -0.75 | 2.3 |
| 2020 | 18.4 | -1.50 | -2.2 |
| 2021 | 28.7 | 0.00 | 6.1 |
| 2022 | -18.1 | 4.25 | 2.5 |
| 2023 | 26.3 | 1.00 | 2.9 |
| 2024 | 25.0 | -1.00 | 2.8 |

What the numbers say, and do not say. The change in the Fed funds rate has a strong negative correlation with stock returns (-0.80), and the change in the 10-year yield is also negative (-0.68). Real GDP growth (0.11), the level of inflation (-0.21) and the change in unemployment (-0.05) show essentially no link at all.

Two warnings keep me honest. First, seven observations is a tiny sample. With so few points, a correlation has to be around 0.75 or more in absolute size to be statistically significant at the usual 5% level, and only the policy rate result clears that bar (p = 0.03). Second, that result leans heavily on one year. Remove 2022, when the Fed raised rates by 4.25 points and stocks fell 18%, and the correlation with the policy rate drops to -0.41, and the one with the 10-year yield to -0.12. The rank correlations in the last column, which are less sensitive to extreme years, are also much weaker (about -0.4). So the safest reading is that rate shocks have hurt stocks when they were large and fast, and that growth, inflation and jobs data on their own do not explain annual returns. This fits the episode table: 2020 had the worst growth number of the period and one of the better stock returns.

There is a practical lesson in this. If the data does not predict returns directly, the useful question is what the data will make the Fed do, and whether that is already priced in.

